## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in edge proxying, per-tenant isolation, simple sidecar patterns, and in-memory caching with TTL. It also exercises docker-compose bootstrapping, TLS termination, and cache invalidation on 5xx.

## Key Concepts
- In-memory TTL cache and eviction
- Per-tenant cache keys and header reliance
- Sidecar design that intercepts requests
- TLS termination at an edge proxy and routing by tenant
- Cache invalidation on error responses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple in-memory TTL cache for GET responses
const cache = new Map();
function cacheMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  if (req.method !== 'GET') return next();
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'] || 'default';
  const key = `${tenant}|${req.originalUrl}`;
  const now = Date.now();
  const entry = cache.get(key);
  if (entry && now < entry.expires) {
    res.set(entry.headers);
    return res.end(entry.body);
  }
  const origSend = res.send.bind(res);
  res.send = (body) => {
    cache.set(key, { body, expires: now + 60000, headers: res.getHeaders() });
    return origSend(body);
  };
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this cache across multiple instances?
- How would you protect against unbounded memory growth?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to add non-intrusive QA in production, teaches traffic manipulation without risking user impact, and validates multi-DC deployment awareness.

## Key Concepts
- Traffic shadowing vs canary releases
- Nginx mirror and upstream configuration
- Docker-DC/edge proxy routing across DCs
- TLS edge termination and tenant isolation

## Code Example
```nginx
# Example shadow config snippet
upstream prod_api { server prod-api:8080; }
upstream staging_api { server staging-api:8080; }
server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://prod_api;
    mirror /mirror_to_staging;
  }
  location = /mirror_to_staging {
    proxy_pass http://staging_api;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor shadowed traffic to ensure no unseen side effects?
- How would you prevent skew if tenant_id headers can be spoofed?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement data governance and privacy in a multi-DC setup: edge TLS, per-tenant policy, hot-reload, and observability with Loki, all integrated via docker-dca.

## Key Concepts

- Traefik edge TLS termination across DCs
- Middleware-based response redaction per-tenant
- Docker Config hot-reload and policy delivery
- Loki-based centralized logs with per-tenant retention
- X-Tenant routing and impact on latency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express-style middleware skeleton for redacting response payloads per-tenant policy
function redactor(policy) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    const oldSend = res.send;
    res.send = function (body) {
      let payload = typeof body === 'string' ? JSON.parse(body) : body;
      const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
      const p = policy[tenant] || { redact: [] };
      if (payload && typeof payload === 'object') {
        for (const key of p.redact) {
          if (payload.hasOwnProperty(key)) payload[key] = 'REDACTED';
        }
      }
      oldSend.call(this, JSON.stringify(payload));
    };
    next();
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test hot-reload correctness and no-downtime guarantees for policy updates?
- How would you measure and mitigate the performance impact of redaction on large payloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine TLS edge proxying with per-tenant feature gates loaded from a daemon-managed config source. It also checks bootstrap discipline in a two-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Configs for runtime config
- TLS termination at Nginx edge proxy
- Per-tenant feature gates in a small API
- Simple bootstrap for tenants and flags

## Code Example
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const yaml = require('js-yaml');
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
let flags = {};
function loadFlags() {
  const txt = fs.readFileSync('/etc/feature-flags.yaml','utf8');
  flags = yaml.load(txt) || {};
}
loadFlags();
app.get('/health-quiz', (req, res) => {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (tenant && flags[tenant] && flags[tenant].enabled) res.sendStatus(200);
  else res.sendStatus(403);
});
app.listen(3000);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this gating with CI and two data centers?
- What are failure modes if the config-loader container is unavailable?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce data residency and leakage controls at runtime with low impact, using Vault for policy and identity, and blue/green rollouts for safe deployments.

## Key Concepts
- Data-loss prevention in containerized traffic
- Vault integration and SPIFFE SVID rotation
- Tenant-scoped JSON schemas
- Dynamic policy updates and hot-reload
- Canary/Blue-green deployment patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal pseudo sidecar validator
const Validator = require('jsonschema').Validator;
const v = new Validator();
const tenant = process.env.TENANT_HEADER; // from request header
const schema = fetchVaultSchema(tenant); // resolves /secret/tenants/{tenant}/schema
v.addSchema(schema, '/tenant/schema');
const payload = getOutboundBody();
const result = v.validate(payload, { // simplified schema
  type: 'object', properties: { tenant: {type: 'string'}, data: {type: 'object'} }, required: ['tenant','data']
}, '/tenant/schema');
if (!result.valid) blockOrRedact(payload, result.errors);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test false positives/negatives per tenant?
- How would you handle schema evolution without breaking existing tenants?
